date-released: "2026-10-23"
identifiers:
  - type: doi
    value: "10.5281/zenodo.23582642"
    description: "Zenodo article DOI"
abstract: >-
  We study finite records whose accuracy for an infinite-dimensional nonlinear
  evolution can be checked without rerunning the producing algorithm. The
  model is a fixed Neumann reaction–diffusion equation with quadratic reaction
  on a fixed time interval. Inputs are rational cosine polynomials satisfying
  a common exponentially weighted coefficient bound. Exact rational tests of
  continuity and coefficient bounds together with the initial discrepancy and
  Galerkin residual certify the trajectory error for the original equation and
  the error of its terminal mean and point value. Acceptance depends on the
  submitted record independently of the algorithm that produced its
  coefficients. A rational Taylor construction yields an accepted record for
  every admissible input and requested accuracy. Construction cost and record
  length are polynomial in the complete input length and the number of
  requested accuracy bits. Checking is polynomial in the input and submitted
  record lengths. The proof controls quantized restarts and the bit lengths of
  intermediate numerators and denominators. A separate rational check
  certifies quadratic accounts with positive transport defects and signed
  injection and approximation increments. Finite response approximations also
  include operator-tail and rounding bounds in the acceptance test. These
  results give a computational realization of the generated spectral framework
  for the encoded Neumann family. A rational finite-dimensional system
  provides a second realization through quantized Picard iteration.
  Construction and checking programs accompany both models.
keywords:
  - "Validated numerics"
  - "Spectral Galerkin methods"
  - "Semilinear parabolic equations"
  - "Reaction-diffusion equations"
  - "A posteriori error estimates"
  - "Exact rational arithmetic"
  - "Proof-carrying computation"
  - "Bit complexity"
